sequenceDiagram
  %% Conn thread index (Conn plan slices O1 and O2, docs/reference/conn.md#thread-index): the Gateway keeps one row per thread of each container T3 server, so a device finds a Project's threads without connecting to every server, and connects only to the server of the thread it opens. The Gateway only reads, as the idle sleep's read does: no Operation, no Swamp run, no Node work. Host servers have no index.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · minute poll, GET /conn/threads, POST /conn/environments/{environment_id}/threads
  %% kind: conn.thread_index
  autonumber
  %% lane C icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane T3 icon=server pill=target tone=purple
  participant C as API caller<br/>a device · MCP
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Conn layer
  participant T3 as T3 server<br/>in the container
  Note over GW: Poll running servers<br/>conn:threads:read · every minute
  %%> When:
  %%> - every minute while the operator switch `ORBIT_CONN_THREAD_INDEX_POLL` is on and Conn is enabled; off by default
  %%> - a unique job on the `problems` queue, behind task work; never on `operations`
  %%> Which servers:
  %%> - every container T3 server whose Instance runs
  %%> Skips, with the reason on the server's record:
  %%> - an asleep or stopped Instance, `conn.environment_asleep`: it is not read and keeps its rows
  %%> - an ended session, `conn.session_expired`: its renewal comes first
  GW->>T3: Read every shell at once<br/>GET /api/orchestration/shell
  %%> How:
  %%> - the admin session the Gateway holds and renews, with `x-t3-orchestration-protocol: 2`
  %%> - all servers in one pool; each has 3 seconds to connect and 10 in total
  %%> On failure:
  %%> - a server that does not answer, refuses or answers nonsense is skipped with `conn.server_unreachable`, `conn.session_rejected`, `conn.request_refused` or `conn.response_invalid`; it keeps its rows and never holds the others
  T3-->>GW: Threads and run state<br/>title · status · pending request
  Note over GW: Write the server's rows<br/>conn_threads
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one row per listed thread: id, title, status, needs input, last user message, last run end, `seen_at`
  %%> - a thread that left the shell leaves the index, unless a write-through recorded it after the read began
  %%> Not stored:
  %%> - message text; only the title
  C->>GW: Record a new thread<br/>POST .../threads
  %%> When:
  %%> - the device started a thread on a container server it paired with
  %%> Requires:
  %%> - node access to the server's Node; a host server is refused with `conn.thread_index_unsupported`
  %%> Also:
  %%> - a task copy's turn records its T3 thread the same way when its projection commits
  GW-->>C: Row recorded<br/>201 · 200 on a repeat
  %%> Result:
  %%> - in the list at once; state stays null until the next read
  C->>GW: List threads<br/>GET /conn/threads?project=
  %%> Requires:
  %%> - node access, as for the server list: only servers whose Node the caller reaches
  %%> Narrows by:
  %%> - `project` or `instance`, both optional
  opt rows older than 10 s
    GW->>T3: Read through<br/>at most 0.5 s · switch on
    %%> When:
    %%> - only while the switch is on, for a running server whose last read is older than 10 seconds
    %%> On failure:
    %%> - the server keeps its rows and records the reason; the list does not try it again within 10 seconds
    T3-->>GW: Threads and run state
  end
  GW-->>C: Rows with seen_at<br/>meta.environments
  %%> Returns:
  %%> - the rows, newest user message first, and each covered server with `power_state`, `read_at` and `read_error`
  %%> - an asleep server's rows are its last read, with their `seen_at`
  Note over GW: Last read before a sleep<br/>instance.sleep admission
  %%> When:
  %%> - before every sleep is admitted, from the idle sweep and from `instance:sleep`; the sleep switch does not matter, the index switch does
  %%> - outside the Instance lock, with the poll's bounds; a failed read keeps the rows and does not stop the sleep
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the asleep Instance keeps these rows until it wakes; destroying it removes them with its server
